Backport of upstream NUT commit bfbba15928aa6a91b3e4b8943e0cad16199d9d48
(NUT issues #598, #993, #2453) for a teardown deadlock that can hang a USB
driver indefinitely on any reconnect. No CVE was assigned, so this patch
has no VEX entry and no SBOM analysis entry.

v2.8.5 calls libusb_init(NULL) on every nut_libusb_open() and
libusb_exit(NULL) on every nut_libusb_close(), so the default context's
refcount rises and falls once per open/close cycle. libusb documents
libusb_exit as a call for after all devices are closed and before the
process terminates. When the refcount reaches zero, libusb tears the
context down and waits on its internal synchronization primitives for
outstanding URBs to drain. URBs orphaned by libusb_reset_device or by an
unexpected device disconnect never drain, so that wait never returns.

Why this matters for this image: the driver hangs while trying to
reconnect, which leaves the UPS unmonitored. Low-battery detection and
the shutdown-on-critical path both stop working, and the container's
comms watchdog is what eventually kills and restarts the driver, so the
symptom is a repeating stale-data window rather than a clean reconnect.
A deployment that passes the USB bus with a static `devices:` mapping
instead of a live bind gets no recovery at all. This image ships every
NUT USB driver, and the bug is in the shared libusb-1.0 code path, so
the exposure is every USB device this image can drive, not one vendor.
Upstream reproduced it on the 0665:5161 Cypress USB-serial bridge family
(Salicru SPS, Ippon, ViewPower, Voltronic Power).

The fix takes an explicit libusb context owned end to end: initialize it
once on the first open, drop libusb_exit() from the close path so closing
only calls the non-blocking libusb_close(), pass the explicit context to
libusb_get_device_list() so the default context is never touched, and
release it exactly once at process exit through an atexit handler bounded
by a 2 second SIGALRM so a wedged teardown cannot block a supervisor's
stop sequence.

The new atexit handler is safe across the driver's backgrounding fork in
this version: common/common.c background() has the parent call
_exit(EXIT_SUCCESS), which does not run atexit handlers, so only the
surviving child ever tears the context down.

Deliberately NOT taken: the companion commit a4813f868 / 9eb4ed46, which
escalates a persistent LIBUSB_ERROR_OVERFLOW in nutdrv_qx to a USB reset.
That change increases how often a reset happens, which is what opens this
deadlock window in the first place, and it is specific to one device
family. Taking the deadlock fix without the extra resets is the
conservative half of the pair.

Verification signal after deploy: the driver banner reports the USB
communication driver version, so a patched image logs
"USB communication driver (libusb 1.0) 0.54" where v2.8.5 logs 0.53, and
upsc reports the same value in driver.version.internal's sibling fields.

Upstream currency: since this commit landed on master (2026-05-24) the
only other change to drivers/libusb1.c is the rdlens bounds fix carried
by patches/libusb-rdlens-oob-read.patch, so there are no follow-up
corrections to fold in.

Only the drivers/libusb1.c hunks of the upstream commit are included,
which is the whole commit; it touched no other file. libusb0.c has a
different structure upstream and is not part of this change, and this
image compiles the libusb-1.0 backend anyway.

Remove this patch when NUT_VERSION moves to v2.8.6+, which ships the fix.
Removal spans three coupled sites: this file, the Dockerfile COPY/apply
step, and the README's "Dependency CVE posture" paragraph that names the
carried backports. See CONTRIBUTING ("Upstream sources may carry
checked-in patches").

diff --git a/drivers/libusb1.c b/drivers/libusb1.c
index 955ada9..b02da58 100644
--- a/drivers/libusb1.c
+++ b/drivers/libusb1.c
@@ -40,9 +40,14 @@
 #include "usb-common.h"
 #include "nut_libusb.h"
 #include "nut_stdint.h"
+#include "nut_bool.h"
+
+#ifndef WIN32
+# include <signal.h>	/* sigaction(), SIGALRM for the atexit watchdog */
+#endif
 
 #define USB_DRIVER_NAME		"USB communication driver (libusb 1.0)"
-#define USB_DRIVER_VERSION	"0.53"
+#define USB_DRIVER_VERSION	"0.54"
 
 /* driver description structure */
 upsdrv_info_t comm_upsdrv_info = {
@@ -55,6 +60,77 @@ upsdrv_info_t comm_upsdrv_info = {
 
 #define MAX_REPORT_SIZE         0x1800
 
+/* Explicit libusb context, initialized once on the first call to
+ * nut_libusb_open() and released exactly once at process shutdown via
+ * the atexit handler below.
+ *
+ * Background: the original code called libusb_init(NULL) on every open and
+ * libusb_exit(NULL) on every close, bumping/decrementing the default
+ * context's refcount. That pattern violates the documented contract for
+ * libusb_exit (which "should be called after closing all open devices and
+ * before your application terminates"), and on certain firmware can wedge
+ * indefinitely: when libusb_exit hits the 1->0 transition it tears the
+ * context down, which waits on its internal sync primitives for URBs to
+ * drain -- but URBs orphaned by libusb_reset_device or by an unexpected
+ * USB device disconnect never drain, so the wait never returns. That
+ * deadlock has been observed on the 0665:5161 Cypress USB-serial bridge
+ * family (Salicru SPS, Ippon, ViewPower, various Voltronic Power UPSes;
+ * see NUT issues #598, #993, #2453) but the underlying bug class is
+ * generic and reachable via any reconnect path.
+ *
+ * The fix is to use an explicit context that we own end-to-end:
+ * initialize it once at first open, never call libusb_exit during runtime
+ * (close_dev no longer does), and release it exactly once at process
+ * shutdown via atexit. A SIGALRM-bounded timeout on the atexit libusb_exit
+ * keeps a deadlock-prone teardown from wedging supervisor shutdowns
+ * (e.g. systemctl stop) if it ever happens at exit time. Generalizes the
+ * 2018 partial fix in commit 4f84b7f92 ("don't libusb_exit() when closing
+ * a previously opened device").
+ */
+static libusb_context	*nut_usb_ctx = NULL;
+static nut_bool_t	 nut_usb_ctx_initialized = false;
+
+#ifndef WIN32
+__attribute__((noreturn))
+static void nut_libusb_cleanup_alarm(int sig)
+{
+	NUT_UNUSED_VARIABLE(sig);
+	/* libusb_exit is taking too long, almost certainly waiting on URBs
+	 * orphaned by a recent device reset / disconnect that will never
+	 * drain. Force the process down so supervisor shutdown isn't blocked.
+	 */
+	_exit(0);
+}
+#endif	/* !WIN32 */
+
+/* Release the libusb context that nut_libusb_open() initialized.
+ * Bounded by a SIGALRM (POSIX) so deadlock-prone teardown can't wedge
+ * process shutdown indefinitely.
+ */
+static void nut_libusb_cleanup_atexit(void)
+{
+	if (!nut_usb_ctx_initialized || !nut_usb_ctx) {
+		return;
+	}
+#ifndef WIN32
+	{
+		struct sigaction	sa, prev;
+		memset(&sa, 0, sizeof(sa));
+		sa.sa_handler = nut_libusb_cleanup_alarm;
+		sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
+		sigaction(SIGALRM, &sa, &prev);
+		alarm(2);	/* sub-ms on healthy context; 2s is generous */
+		libusb_exit(nut_usb_ctx);
+		alarm(0);
+		sigaction(SIGALRM, &prev, NULL);
+	}
+#else	/* WIN32 */
+	libusb_exit(nut_usb_ctx);
+#endif	/* !WIN32 */
+	nut_usb_ctx = NULL;
+	nut_usb_ctx_initialized = false;
+}
+
 static void nut_libusb_close(libusb_device_handle *udev);
 
 /*! Add USB-related driver variables with addvar() and dstate_setinfo().
@@ -291,10 +367,18 @@ static int nut_libusb_open(libusb_device_handle **udevp,
 		usb_hid_number_opts_parsed = 1;
 	}
 
-	/* libusb base init */
-	if (libusb_init(NULL) < 0) {
-		libusb_exit(NULL);
-		fatal_with_errno(EXIT_FAILURE, "Failed to init libusb 1.0");
+	/* libusb base init: initialize our explicit context on the first call
+	 * here, and register the matching one-shot teardown via atexit. See the
+	 * comment near the file-scope declaration of nut_usb_ctx for the design
+	 * rationale (avoids the libusb_exit-per-reconnect misuse and the
+	 * teardown-deadlock it enables on some firmware).
+	 */
+	if (!nut_usb_ctx_initialized) {
+		if (libusb_init(&nut_usb_ctx) < 0) {
+			fatal_with_errno(EXIT_FAILURE, "Failed to init libusb 1.0");
+		}
+		nut_usb_ctx_initialized = true;
+		atexit(nut_libusb_cleanup_atexit);
 	}
 
 /* TODO: Find a place for this, from Windows branch made for libusb0.c */
@@ -312,7 +396,7 @@ static int nut_libusb_open(libusb_device_handle **udevp,
 		libusb_close(*udevp);
 #endif
 
-	devcount = libusb_get_device_list(NULL, &devlist);
+	devcount = libusb_get_device_list(nut_usb_ctx, &devlist);
 
 	/* devcount may be < 0, loop will get skipped;
 	 * its SSIZE_MAX < SIZE_MAX for devnum */
@@ -1166,7 +1250,12 @@ static void nut_libusb_close(libusb_device_handle *udev)
 	 */
 	/* libusb_release_interface(udev, usb_subdriver.hid_rep_index); */
 	libusb_close(udev);
-	libusb_exit(NULL);
+	/* libusb_exit() is no longer called here. The libusb context is owned
+	 * by nut_libusb_open() and released exactly once at process shutdown
+	 * via the atexit handler; calling it per-close was a longstanding
+	 * misuse that deadlocked on orphaned URBs after a device reset or
+	 * disconnect (NUT #598). See the comment near nut_usb_ctx above.
+	 */
 }
 
 usb_communication_subdriver_t usb_subdriver = {
